b63be90d72f3a43","eb8e52366d19e8e3","eb8e52366d19e8e3","ec184946d5015eed","edfe60861da442ca","efadb81b9d759471","f0ce3731f2040080","f2778980522d8a11","f2827ba15ca19bb8","f5779139b3b3beaf","f6ec63053e9af54f","f6f410d24cfbf0d0","f8038c35cda5d035","fa9435e4e1400dd4","fc54452f0a598b1a"],"groups":["e942549eb2701268","59ba8a678c44c493","71b2495735e2352d","83390b551c016789","2141b142249e70cc","54249e0b3bc722b2"],"findings":"## Review: timed-out marker and git-failure handling\n\nTests pass (233/233 on the clone). The timed-out marker groups check out. The git-failure group has two verified defects that the annotations do not disclose.\n\n### Mismatches\n\n1. **`git-failure`, `configAt` (h6): the config is silently dropped when git runs from a subdirectory.** **verified.** `ls-tree` resolves paths relative to the current directory, while `git show ref:path` is root-relative. `git()` has no `cwd`, so running `git-gloss signals` from `src/` gives `ls-tree` an empty result. `configAt` then returns null with no warning. The repo's `stricter` pattern was applied from the repo root and dropped from `src/`. The pre-change code (c94117d) still applies it from `src/`, so this is a regression. The TODO's claim that failures \"now warn\" also does not hold here, because this path is the quiet \"no file\" branch. The test suite runs from the root, so it misses this. Fix: add `--full-tree` to the `ls-tree` call. I checked that `git ls-tree --full-tree --name-only main -- .gloss/config.json` returns the path from `src/`. Add a test that runs from a subdirectory.\n\n2. **`git-failure`, `trustedRef` (h10): a broken ref now stops the base search instead of falling through.** **verified.** The old `gitTry` loop moved on to the next candidate on any failure. The new code warns and returns null on the first failing `merge-base`. Example: `refs/remotes/origin/main` points at a tree and `refs/heads/main` holds the real config. The pre-change code used `main`'s config. The new code warns \"git failed finding the base branch\" and applies only the defaults, so the stricter pattern is lost. The notes and TODO do not mention that a broken `origin/main` now disables the local `main` fallback. Either keep trying later refs and warn, or document the change.\n\n### Misgraded necessity\n\n- `git-failure` [required] is fine, since it is the TODO task. But its notes (\"warns once and the defaults apply instead of silent 'no config'\") overstate it given finding 1.\n- `timeout-marker` [required]: the core marker is required. The `floorFrom` sort (h20/h21 region) that puts timed-out reasons first is a presentation tweak. The notes justify it, so I would keep it, but it is not required by the task. **read**\n- The `timedOut` list in `Analysis` (h8/h17 and the client card) is required for show and the card, as the notes say. It can duplicate the floor reason line in `signals` and `show` when the floor beats the declared level. That duplication is cosmetic. **read**\n\n### Removal candidates\n\n- None are clearly removable. Removing the `timedOutNotes` list would bring back the show-lost-mention problem the author describes.\n\n### Simplifications\n\n- For finding 1, the one-line `--full-tree` fix removes the need for the ls-tree-vs-show split. A single `git cat-file -e ref:path` probe would also work, but `--full-tree` is the smaller change.\n- `gitAnswer` plus the `/failed$/` message match is brittle: it depends on git's message format for \"no answer\" with exit 1. Matching on the status alone, with an empty stderr, would be clearer. **read**\n\n### Missing risks\n\n- A subdirectory invocation silently loses the repo's stricter config (finding 1). This is the most serious risk for users who run the CLI from anywhere but the root.\n- A broken remote-tracking ref disables the local-branch fallback (finding 2).\n- The git-failure test uses a loose-object delete under `.git/objects`. It only covers the blob-missing case. It does not cover a subdirectory or a broken ref.\n\n### Notes on accuracy\n\n- The `timeout-marker` claims check out: `matchLines` returns `timedOut` for skipped-set calls, damped hits are excluded from `timedOutNotes`, and the test covers `--json`, the floor reason, `show` and the html data. **read** (the test passes, verified by the suite run)\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
