in `trustedRef`. A non-1 exit there (status 128) propagates out of `readRepoConfig`. I tried to trigger it: a corrupt `.git/HEAD` makes the repo undiscoverable before config is read, and a ref pointing at a missing object did not reach the check. So the path is real in the code but I could not construct an input that hits it. Wrapping that call in the same handling as the candidate loop would make the claim true.\n\n2. **`timedOutNotes` does more than its comment says (read).** The comment says it lists timed-out patterns \"on a hit that counts toward the floor\". The code lists every non-damped timed-out hit in every hunk. That includes a pattern whose class is below the floor, and hits on lines no group claims. The card and `show` can then say \"counted as matching\" for a pattern that did not affect the floor. Either filter to hits whose class sets the floor, or fix the comment and the attention wording to match.\n\n### Misgraded necessity\n\n3. **`git-failure` is correctly `required`**, since the task asks for it. `tests` as `supporting` is also fine. I see no group that is really incidental.\n\n### Removal candidates\n\nNone worth removing. Every group is either the task or the tests for it. The `GitError.status` field is only used by `gitAnswer`. It stays if `gitAnswer` stays.\n\n### Simplifications\n\n4. **`gitAnswer` sniffs the message it says it no longer sniffs (read).** The notes say earlier versions sniffed git's message and that this was dropped. Yet `gitAnswer` still matches `/failed$/` on the message to detect \"empty stderr\". The `status === 1` check plus a flag for \"stderr was empty\" would be direct, and would match the design described in the notes.\n\n5. **`trustedRef` returns through a comma expression (read, minor).** `failed ? (console.error(...), null) : noBase()` is hard to read. A small `noBaseFailed()` helper, next to `noBase()`, would match the rest of the file.\n\n### Missing risks\n\n- **Wall-clock test.** The \"timed out\" test relies on the 250 ms budget. It is effectively deterministic for `(a+)+$` on 40 characters, but the marker's semantics still depend on machine speed. The TODO already notes this.\n- **Extra git process per config read.** `ls-tree` adds one process per command. Config is cached per cwd, so the cost is negligible.\n- **Additive JSON field.** `timedOut` is a new optional key in `--json` output. Consumers that are strict about schemas may need to know.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES\n\nThe full suite passes: 236 of 236 tests, exit 0. The earlier review said the full suite was unconfirmed; that is now resolved.\n\nThe findings in my report stand unchanged:\n\n1. **Read, not reproduced:** `trustedRef`'s first `rev-parse HEAD` call sits outside the `try`, so a git failure there can still throw instead of warning and using defaults.\n2. **Read:** `timedOutNotes` lists every non-damped timed-out hit, not only those that set the floor. Its comment and the attention wording overstate what it does.\n3. **Read:** `gitAnswer` still matches the error message, which contradicts the design the notes describe.\n4. **Read, minor:** the comma expression in `trustedRef` is hard to read.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
