3be90d72f3a43","eb8e52366d19e8e3","eb8e52366d19e8e3","ec184946d5015eed","edfe60861da442ca","efadb81b9d759471","f0ce3731f2040080","f2778980522d8a11","f2827ba15ca19bb8","f5779139b3b3beaf","f6ec63053e9af54f","f6f410d24cfbf0d0","f8038c35cda5d035","fa9435e4e1400dd4","fc54452f0a598b1a"],"groups":["e942549eb2701268","59ba8a678c44c493","71b2495735e2352d","83390b551c016789","2141b142249e70cc","54249e0b3bc722b2"],"findings":"The review found one major defect, which I confirmed by running git directly. I did not run the project's test suite.\n\n## 1. Major, verified: the repo config is silently ignored when run from a subdirectory\n**Where:** `src/config.ts`, `configAt` (the new `git ls-tree --name-only ref -- .gloss/config.json` check).\n\n`git ls-tree` resolves its pathspec relative to the current directory. `git show ref:path` resolves from the repo root. The code never changes directory, so it uses whatever directory the user ran it from.\n\n**Scenario:** The base branch has `.gloss/config.json`. The user runs `git gloss signals`, `show` or `html` from `repo/sub/`.\n- `ls-tree` looks for `sub/.gloss/config.json`, finds nothing, and prints nothing, so `configAt` returns `null`.\n- The repo's own patterns and path rules are dropped, with no warning, so the floor can come out lower than it should.\n- Before this change the code ran `git show ref:.gloss/config.json` directly, which worked from any directory.\n\n**Check I ran:** In a scratch repo with the config committed, I ran both commands from `sub/`.\n- `ls-tree` printed 0 bytes.\n- `git show HEAD:.gloss/config.json` printed `{}`.\n\n**Why the tests miss it:** Every new test runs from the repo root.\n\n**Fix:** Pass `--full-tree` to `ls-tree`, or drop the `ls-tree` presence check and distinguish a missing file from a git failure another way.\n\n## 2. Minor, read: an unborn HEAD now gets a misleading warning\n**Where:** `src/config.ts`, `trustedRef`.\n\nIn a repo with no commits, `merge-base HEAD <ref>` exits 128. That now takes the \"git failed finding the base branch\" warning path instead of the old quiet \"no base branch\" one.\n\nIf `rev-parse --verify` succeeds for a ref that exists, the same failure appears, only worded differently. This is a cosmetic regression in a rare state.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
